steps.body:
You already chain `<<` to print literals. Variables chain the same way: each `<<` hands the next piece to `std::cout`, and the pieces print back to back in order.

```cpp
std::string city = "Oslo";
int temp = 12;
std::cout << city << " is " << temp << " degrees\n";
```

That prints `Oslo is 12 degrees`. `std::cout` never adds spaces for you, so put them inside the string pieces. Forget them and you get `Oslois12degrees`.

One statement can mix every type you know: a `std::string`, an `int`, a `double`, and a `char` each print in their natural form. A `char` such as `'$'` prints as the character itself, so it is a handy way to glue a symbol right onto a number with no gap.

```cpp
char unit = 'C';
std::cout << temp << unit << '\n';  // 12C
```

A `double` such as `1.68` prints as `1.68` here because its default form is short. The next part shows what happens with longer numbers.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::string name = "Ada";
    int age = 36;
    double height = 1.68;

    std::cout << name << " is " << age << " years old and " << height << " m tall.\n";
    return 0;
}

